<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened Runtime entitlements for the notarised macOS build. See SIGNING.md.

  THIS FILE IS UNUSED UNLESS $SQUIDMIP_CODESIGN_IDENTITY IS SET. With no identity the bundle is
  ad-hoc signed (which is what macOS already requires of any arm64 binary) and entitlements are
  meaningless, so scripts/hcs-viewer.spec passes entitlements_file=None in that case.

  UNVERIFIED, and it has to be said rather than implied: this file has NEVER been used in a real
  signing run, because signing needs an Apple Developer ID that is not on this machine. It is a
  STARTING POINT derived from what a frozen CPython + Qt application generally needs under the
  Hardened Runtime, not a tested result.

  LEAST PRIVILEGE, and how to actually get there. Every entitlement below is a hole in the
  Hardened Runtime, so the correct procedure is to REMOVE them, not to trust them:

    1. Comment out all three, sign, notarise, and run the app on a machine that has never seen it.
    2. Add back ONLY the one whose absence produced a real, reproduced crash, and write the crash
       down next to it.
    3. Ship the shortest list that works.

  Do not skip step 1 because the app is large. An app that ships with
  disable-library-validation it did not need has permanently widened its own attack surface to
  save one build cycle.

  There is deliberately NO com.apple.security.app-sandbox key here. This app opens an acquisition
  folder ANYWHERE on the user's disk, chosen at runtime, and a sandboxed app cannot do that
  without security-scoped bookmarks the code does not implement. Notarisation does not require the
  sandbox; only the Mac App Store does, and this app is not distributed that way.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- CPython writes and then executes code in memory in several places (ctypes closures, and
         any JIT in the dependency set). Without this, the Hardened Runtime kills the process. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- vispy/PyOpenGL compile shaders at runtime and Qt's QML/JS engines JIT. Try removing this
         one FIRST: it is the likeliest of the three to be unnecessary for this app, which uses
         Qt Widgets rather than QML. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- PyInstaller's onedir layout dlopen()s ~2000 bundled .so/.dylib files at runtime. When
         every one of them is signed with the SAME Developer ID (which `codesign --deep` or a
         per-binary signing pass does), library validation is satisfied and THIS KEY SHOULD BE
         DELETED. Keep it only if you have measured a "code signature ... not valid for use in
         process" abort with it removed. It is the widest of the three. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
